i=A.bI(A.a1(["Python","def binary_search(arr, target):\n    low = 0\n    high = len(arr) - 1\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        # Prevent potential overflow in other languages, good habit\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n        \n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n            \n    return -1\n","Java","int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n    int low = 0;\n    int high = arr.length - 1;\n    \n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        \n        if (arr[mid] == target) {\n            return mid;\n        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n","C++","int binarySearch(const vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n    int low = 0;\n    int high = arr.size() - 1;\n    \n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        \n        if (arr[mid] == target) {\n            return mid;\n        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n","JavaScript","function binarySearch(arr, target) {\n    let low = 0;\n    let high = arr.length - 1;\n    \n    while (low <= high) {\n        let mid = Math.floor(low + (high - low) / 2);\n        \n        if (arr[mid] === target) {\n            return mid;\n        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n"],h,h),p,q,"Master the divide-and-conquer strategy! Learn how Binary Search slashes your search space in half with every single step, turning an O(n) linear drag into a lightning-fast O(log n) operation. We will explore midpoints, boundary mechanics, and critical index edge cases.","algorithm-binary-search",m,"Binary Search",A.a(["Integer Overflow: Calculating mid as '(low + high) / 2' can overflow the maximum integer limit in languages like Java/C++ if the array is massive.","Infinite Loops: Forgetting to update pointers with '+ 1' or '- 1' (e.g., setting 'low = mid') can trap the loop forever if the target isn't found.","Unsorted Data: Running binary search on unsorted data yields completely unpredictable results without throwing a runtime error."],l),i,A.a([A.H(1,"Binary Search relies entirely on the property that data is sorted. Eliminating half of the search space at each step is only logical if we know every element past the midpoint is larger (or smaller) than our target.",A.a(["The dataset must be stored in a Linked List.","The elements in the dataset must be sorted.","The dataset must contain only unique positive integers.","The size of the dataset must be an even number."],l),"What is the absolute fundamental prerequisite for running a Binary Search on a dataset?"),A.H(2,"If 'low' and 'high' are both exceptionally large integers, adding them together directly via '(low + high)' can exceed the maximum capacity of a standard 32-bit integer, resulting in overflow and bugs. The alternative math bypasses this risk safely.",A.a(["It executes fewer CPU cycles.","It yields a more precise decimal value.","It prevents potential integer overflow errors when handling very large arrays.","It forces the compiler to auto-optimize the search block."],l),"Why is calculating 'int mid = low + (high - low) / 2' preferred over 'int mid = (low + high) / 2' in languages like Java and C++?"),A.H(2,"Because Binary Search cuts the search space in half with each iteration, its efficiency scales logarithmically. Log base 2 of 1,024 is exactly 10 (2^{10} = 1024), meaning it takes a maximum of 10 checks.",A.a(["512","1,024","10","1"],l),"If a sorted array contains 1,024 elements, what is the maximum number of comparisons a Binary Search will make to find an element or prove it doesn't exist?"),A.H(2,"If the target is smaller than the middle element, it must reside in the left half of our sorted space. Therefore, we discard the right half entirely by shifting our upper boundary ('high') down to 'mid - 1'.",A.a(["The 'low' pointer moves to 'mid + 1'.","Both pointers remain exactly where they are.","The 'high' pointer moves to 'mid - 1'.","The search terminates immediately as a failure."],l),"What happens to the 'low' and 'high' pointers if the target value is SMALLER than the current middle element?")],g),"assets/mds/dsa/algorithms/binary.md",B.JU)
