j=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'# Python natively supports Hash Maps via Dictionaries\ndef hash_map_demo():\n    # Initialize an empty dictionary\n    student_scores = {}\n    \n    # Insertion (O(1)) - Mapping a String Key to an Integer Value\n    student_scores["Alice"] = 95\n    student_scores["Bob"] = 82\n    \n    # Update existing key\n    student_scores["Alice"] = 98 \n    \n    # Safe Retrieval (O(1))\n    score = student_scores.get("Alice", -1) # Returns 98\n    missing = student_scores.get("Charlie", -1) # Returns -1 (Default)\n    \n    # Deletion (O(1))\n    if "Bob" in student_scores:\n        del student_scores["Bob"]\n        \n    for key, val in student_scores.items():\n        print(f"{key}: {val}")\n',"Java",'import java.util.HashMap;\n\nclass HashMapDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Initialize a HashMap mapping Strings to Integers\n        HashMap<String, Integer> studentScores = new HashMap<>();\n        \n        // Insertion & Updating (O(1))\n        studentScores.put("Alice", 95);\n        studentScores.put("Bob", 82);\n        studentScores.put("Alice", 98); // Overwrites 95\n        \n        // Safe Retrieval (O(1))\n        int score = studentScores.getOrDefault("Alice", -1); // 98\n        int missing = studentScores.getOrDefault("Charlie", -1); // -1\n        \n        // Deletion (O(1))\n        studentScores.remove("Bob");\n        \n        // Iteration\n        for (String key : studentScores.keySet()) {\n            System.out.println(key + ": " + studentScores.get(key));\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_map>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid hashMapDemo() {\n    // Initialize an unordered_map (C++ implementation of Hash Map)\n    unordered_map<string, int> studentScores;\n    \n    // Insertion & Updating (O(1))\n    studentScores["Alice"] = 95;\n    studentScores["Bob"] = 82;\n    studentScores["Alice"] = 98; // Overwrites 95\n    \n    // Retrieval (O(1))\n    if (studentScores.find("Alice") != studentScores.end()) {\n        cout << "Alice: " << studentScores["Alice"] << endl;\n    }\n    \n    // Deletion (O(1))\n    studentScores.erase("Bob");\n    \n    // Iteration\n    for (auto const& [key, val] : studentScores) {\n        cout << key << ": " << val << endl;\n    }\n}\n',"JavaScript",'function hashMapDemo() {\n    // JS natively supports Hash Maps via the Map object \n    // (Standard {} objects are also used, but Map is preferred for strict Key-Value)\n    const studentScores = new Map();\n    \n    // Insertion & Updating (O(1))\n    studentScores.set("Alice", 95);\n    studentScores.set("Bob", 82);\n    studentScores.set("Alice", 98); // Overwrites 95\n    \n    // Safe Retrieval (O(1))\n    const score = studentScores.has("Alice") ? studentScores.get("Alice") : -1; // 98\n    const missing = studentScores.has("Charlie") ? studentScores.get("Charlie") : -1; // -1\n    \n    // Deletion (O(1))\n    studentScores.delete("Bob");\n    \n    // Iteration\n    for (let [key, val] of studentScores) {\n        console.log(`${key}: ${val}`);\n    }\n}\n'],i,i),p,q,"Welcome to the Dictionary of the computer world! A Hash Map (or Dictionary) is arguably the most frequently used data structure in modern software engineering. Learn how to map custom 'Keys' to specific 'Values' to build incredibly fast frequency counters, caches, and lookup tables that operate in instant O(1) time.","hash-maps",n,"Hash Maps (Dictionaries)",A.a(["Mutable Keys: If you use a mutable object (like an Array or List) as a Key, and then change that object, its hash code changes. The map will never be able to find your value again, creating a severe memory leak.","Missing Key Crashes: Trying to directly access a key that doesn't exist (e.g., 'map[key]') will crash your program in many languages. Always use safe methods like 'getOrDefault()' or check 'containsKey()' first.","The Ordering Illusion: Iterating over a Hash Map will yield key-value pairs in a seemingly random order depending on the hash math. If you need sequential order, you must use specialized structures like a LinkedHashMap or TreeMap."],m),j,A.a([A.H(1,"Hash Maps free you from the constraint of numeric indexes. By hashing custom keys (like Strings), a Hash Map lets you build associative relationships (like 'Alice' -> 95) while maintaining the instant lookup speed of an array.",A.a(["Arrays can only hold strings, while Hash Maps can only hold integers.","Arrays require you to access data using sequential, numeric integer indexes (0, 1, 2), while Hash Maps allow you to access data using custom, human-readable Keys (like 'Username' or 'Email').","Arrays have O(1) retrieval time, while Hash Maps have O(n) retrieval time.","Arrays store data on the hard drive, while Hash Maps store data in the CPU cache."],m),"What is the fundamental difference between accessing data in a standard Array versus a Hash Map?"),A.H(3,"Keys in a Hash Map must be completely unique. If you run `map.put('Alice', 95)` and then `map.put('Alice', 98)`, the Hash Map jumps to Alice's bucket and overwrites 95 with 98.",A.a(["The program throws a Runtime Exception and crashes.","The Hash Map keeps both values, creating a duplicate Key.","The Hash Map silently rejects the insertion and keeps the old value.","The new Value overwrites the old Value, permanently updating the Key's associated data."],m),"What happens if you insert a Key-Value pair into a Hash Map, but that specific Key already exists in the map?"),A.H(0,"This is the classic 'Frequency Counter' pattern. As you read each word, you check if it exists as a Key in the Hash Map. If it doesn't, insert it with a Value of 1. If it does, grab its current Value, add 1, and overwrite it. This completes the entire book in O(n) time.",A.a(["Use a Hash Map where the Key is the Word (String) and the Value is the Frequency Count (Integer).","Use a standard Array and sort it alphabetically.","Use a Hash Set to store the words.","Use a Linked List to maintain the chronological order of the words."],m),"You need to write an algorithm that counts exactly how many times every single word appears in a 1,000-page book. What is the most efficient approach?"),A.H(1,"A Key's location in memory is strictly tied to its hash code at the exact moment of insertion. If you change the object later, its hash code changes. When you ask the map to find it, the map will calculate the new hash code, look in a completely different bucket, and return 'Not Found'.",A.a(["Because Hash Maps do not allow objects to be used as Keys.","Because if you modify the object after inserting it, its mathematical hash code will change. The map will look in the wrong bucket and permanently lose access to your Value.","Because mutable objects take up too much memory and will crash the Hash Map.","Because the Values will automatically become mutable as well."],m),"Why is it extremely dangerous to use a mutable (changeable) object, such as a List or an Array, as a Key in a Hash Map?")],h),"assets/mds/dsa/hash/hashmap.md",B.Uu)
