q=A.a(["Best & Average Case: O(1) Time for Enqueue (Insert), Dequeue (Remove), and Peek (View Front)","Worst Case: O(n) Time for Searching (You must dequeue elements until you find the target)","Space Complexity: O(n) (To store the elements)"],k)
i=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'from collections import deque\n\nclass Queue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # We use deque instead of a standard list.\n        # list.pop(0) takes O(n) time, but deque.popleft() takes O(1) time!\n        self.items = deque()\n        \n    def enqueue(self, item):\n        # Add item to the back of the queue\n        self.items.append(item)\n        \n    def dequeue(self):\n        # Remove and return the front item\n        if not self.is_empty():\n            return self.items.popleft()\n        raise IndexError("Queue Underflow: Cannot dequeue from an empty queue")\n        \n    def peek(self):\n        if not self.is_empty():\n            return self.items[0]\n        return None\n        \n    def is_empty(self):\n        return len(self.items) == 0\n',"Java",'import java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.Queue;\nimport java.util.NoSuchElementException;\n\nclass QueueDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Java natively provides a Queue interface, often implemented by LinkedList\n        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n\n        // Enqueue - O(1)\n        queue.add(10);\n        queue.add(20);\n\n        // Peek (View Front) - O(1)\n        System.out.println("Front: " + queue.peek()); // 10\n\n        // Dequeue - O(1)\n        if (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int removed = queue.remove(); \n            System.out.println("Removed: " + removed); // 10\n        } else {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException("Queue is empty!");\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\n#include <queue>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid queueDemo() {\n    // C++ Standard Template Library (STL) provides a highly optimized queue\n    queue<int> q;\n\n    // Enqueue - O(1)\n    q.push(10);\n    q.push(20);\n\n    // Peek at the front - O(1)\n    if (!q.empty()) {\n        cout << "Front: " << q.front() << endl; // 10\n    }\n\n    // Dequeue - O(1)\n    if (!q.empty()) {\n        q.pop(); // Removes 10 from the front\n    } else {\n        throw out_of_range("Queue Underflow");\n    }\n}\n',"JavaScript",'class Queue {\n    constructor() {\n        this.items = {};\n        this.headIndex = 0;\n        this.tailIndex = 0;\n    }\n    \n    // O(1) Enqueue\n    enqueue(item) {\n        this.items[this.tailIndex] = item;\n        this.tailIndex++;\n    }\n    \n    // O(1) Dequeue (Avoids the O(n) array.shift() trap!)\n    dequeue() {\n        if (this.isEmpty()) {\n            throw new Error("Queue Underflow!");\n        }\n        const item = this.items[this.headIndex];\n        delete this.items[this.headIndex];\n        this.headIndex++;\n        return item;\n    }\n    \n    peek() {\n        if (this.isEmpty()) return null;\n        return this.items[this.headIndex];\n    }\n    \n    isEmpty() {\n        return this.headIndex === this.tailIndex;\n    }\n}\n'],g,g),q,h,"Welcome to the line! Master the FIFO (First-In, First-Out) principle. Imagine waiting in line at a movie theater: the first person to get in line is mathematically guaranteed to be the first person to get a ticket. Learn how this incredibly fair data structure powers network traffic buffers, CPU task scheduling, and Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithms.","queue",n,"Queues",A.a(["The Array.shift() Trap: Beginners often use standard Arrays to build Queues. In JavaScript or Python, using 'shift()' or 'pop(0)' to remove the first element forces the computer to physically move every other element one step to the left, destroying your O(1) performance and creating an O(n) bottleneck.","Queue Underflow: Just like a Stack, attempting to Dequeue from an empty Queue will trigger a fatal crash. Always use an 'isEmpty()' check before removing data.","Memory Leaks (Pointer Implementations): If you build a queue using a standard array with 'head' and 'tail' pointers, the array will keep growing infinitely to the right as you add items, even if you dequeue old items from the left. This requires a 'Circular Queue' or Linked List to solve."],k),i,A.a([A.H(1,"A Queue strictly enforces FIFO. Whoever gets in line first is served first. The element that has been in the queue the longest is always the next one to be dequeued.",A.a(["LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) - The last item added is processed first.",m,"Random Access - Any item can be retrieved instantly using an index.","Priority Order - Items are automatically sorted by their numeric value."],k),"What is the defining operational rule of a Queue data structure?"),A.H(2,"A printer uses a Queue to ensure absolute fairness. If Computer A sends a document, and then Computer B sends a document, the printer places them in a Queue so it prints Computer A's document first.",A.a(["The 'Undo' button in a text editor (Ctrl+Z).","A web browser's 'Back' button history.","A network printer receiving print jobs from 10 different computers.","A dictionary application finding the definition of a word."],k),"Which of the following real-world scenarios is almost certainly managed by a Queue?"),A.H(2,"Arrays rely on contiguous memory indexes (0, 1, 2). If you delete index 0, the element at index 1 must physically move to 0, index 2 must move to 1, etc. If the queue has 1,000,000 items, a single dequeue takes 1,000,000 operations!",A.a(["Because it throws a syntax error in modern languages.","Because removing the first element leaves an empty hole at index 0, crashing the array.","Because it forces the computer to manually shift every single remaining element one position to the left, degrading performance from O(1) to O(n).","Because it accidentally removes the last element instead of the first."],k),"Why is using a standard Array's built-in 'remove from front' function (like array.shift() in JS or list.pop(0) in Python) a terrible way to build a Queue?"),A.H(1,"You cannot serve a customer if there is nobody in line! Attempting to remove an item from an empty Queue causes a Queue Underflow exception. You must always check if the queue is empty before dequeueing.",A.a(["The Queue automatically resets its internal pointers to zero.","It triggers a 'Queue Underflow' error, potentially crashing the program.",l,"It triggers a 'Queue Overflow' error."],k),"What happens if you execute a 'Dequeue' operation on a Queue that is completely empty?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/queue/queueinfor.md",B.a4F)
