p=A.a(["Best Case: O(1) Time (If the string starts immediately with a closing bracket, it instantly fails)","Average & Worst Case: O(n) Time (Must scan every character in the string)","Space Complexity: O(n) (If the string is entirely opening brackets, they all get pushed to the stack)"],k)
h=A.bI(A.a1(["Python","def isValid(s: str) -> bool:\n    stack = []\n    # Map closing brackets to their corresponding opening brackets\n    mapping = {')': '(', '}': '{', ']': '['}\n    \n    for char in s:\n        if char in mapping:\n            # Pop the top element if stack isn't empty, else assign a dummy value\n            top_element = stack.pop() if stack else '#'\n            \n            # If the popped element doesn't match the required opening bracket, it's invalid\n            if mapping[char] != top_element:\n                return False\n        else:\n            # It's an opening bracket, push it onto the stack\n            stack.append(char)\n            \n    # If the stack is empty at the end, all brackets were matched!\n    return not stack\n","Java","import java.util.Stack;\n\nclass Solution {\n    public boolean isValid(String s) {\n        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();\n        \n        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {\n            // Push opening brackets\n            if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[') {\n                stack.push(c);\n            } \n            // Handle closing brackets\n            else {\n                if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;\n                \n                char top = stack.pop();\n                if (c == ')' && top != '(') return false;\n                if (c == '}' && top != '{') return false;\n                if (c == ']' && top != '[') return false;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Valid only if no unmatched brackets are left over\n        return stack.isEmpty();\n    }\n}\n","C++","#include <stack>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool isValid(string s) {\n    stack<char> st;\n    \n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[') {\n            st.push(c);\n        } else {\n            if (st.empty()) return false;\n            \n            char top = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n            \n            if (c == ')' && top != '(') return false;\n            if (c == '}' && top != '{') return false;\n            if (c == ']' && top != '[') return false;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    return st.empty();\n}\n","JavaScript","function isValid(s) {\n    const stack = [];\n    const map = {\n        ')': '(',\n        '}': '{',\n        ']': '['\n    };\n    \n    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\n        const char = s[i];\n        \n        // If it's a closing bracket\n        if (map[char]) {\n            const topElement = stack.length > 0 ? stack.pop() : '#';\n            if (map[char] !== topElement) {\n                return false;\n            }\n        } else {\n            // It's an opening bracket\n            stack.push(char);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    return stack.length === 0;\n}\n"],g,g),p,q,"Have you ever wondered how your code editor instantly highlights a missing parenthesis or curly brace and throws a Syntax Error? It's powered by a Stack! Because Stacks follow the LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) rule, they are the perfect data structure for pairing nested items. The most recently opened bracket MUST be the very first bracket to be closed.","stack-app-brackets",n,"Stack Application: Balanced Brackets",A.a(["The Leftover Trap: Beginners often forget to check if the stack is empty at the very end of the loop. If you feed the algorithm '()(', it will match the first two, but leave the last '(' sitting in the stack. You must return `stack.isEmpty()` at the end!","Stack Underflow on Close: If a string starts with a closing bracket like ']', attempting to pop from the stack to check for a match will crash the program. You must explicitly check if the stack is empty before popping.","Pushing the Wrong Thing: The algorithm only works if you push *opening* brackets. If you accidentally push closing brackets, the chronological pairing logic completely breaks down."],k),h,A.a([A.H(1,"When a closing bracket is found, the rule is to instantly check the most recent unmatched opening bracket. Because of LIFO, that opening bracket is sitting right at the top of the Stack. We pop it and compare them.",A.a(["Push the ')' onto the top of the stack.","Pop the top element off the stack (which should be '(') and check if they form a valid pair.","Scan the rest of the string to count how many closing parentheses exist.","Clear the entire stack."],k),"When reading the string '{ [ ( ) ] }', what should the algorithm do when it encounters the first closing parenthesis ')' ?"),A.H(2,"If you have a closing bracket but nothing in the stack, it means you are trying to close a bracket that was never opened! This is structurally invalid, so the algorithm can immediately exit and return False.",A.a(["It means the string is valid, and the algorithm returns True.","It pushes the '}' onto the stack.","It means there is an unmatched closing bracket (e.g., the string is just '}'). The algorithm should instantly return False.","It throws a Stack Overflow error."],k),"What happens if the algorithm encounters a closing bracket '}', but the Stack is completely empty?"),A.H(0,"If the string was '((()', all the pairs will match, but two '(' will be left over in the stack at the end. A valid string must perfectly pair every bracket, meaning the stack must be completely empty when the loop finishes.",A.a(["We check if the Stack is completely empty.","We check if the Stack contains exactly 2 items.","We check if the string length is an even number.","We return True by default."],k),"At the very end of the algorithm, after checking every character in the string, how do we finally determine if the string was valid?"),A.H(1,"Time is O(n) because we scan every character in the string exactly once. Space is O(n) because in the worst-case scenario (a string made entirely of opening brackets), we have to push every single character onto the Stack.",A.a(["O(n\xb2) Time and O(1) Space","O(n) Time and O(n) Space","O(log n) Time and O(n) Space","O(1) Time and O(1) Space"],k),"What is the Time and Space Complexity for this Balanced Brackets algorithm?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/stack/balanced.md",B.ah3)
q=A.a(["Level-by-Level Guarantee: Because a Queue enforces FIFO, nodes closest to the starting point are mathematically guaranteed to be processed before nodes further away.","Shortest Path: In an unweighted graph or maze, BFS is the standard algorithm used to find the absolute shortest path from Point A to Point B.","Systematic Exploration: Unlike the deep, plunging nature of DFS (which uses a Stack), BFS safely sweeps outward like a radar ping."],k)
p=A.a(["High Memory Consumption: The Queue must hold an entire 'level' of nodes at once. If a tree or graph is very wide, the Queue's space complexity becomes O(w), which can cause memory issues.","Slower in Deep Trees: If the target node is hidden at the very bottom of a massive tree, BFS wastes time exploring every single upper branch before finally reaching the bottom."],k)
