a=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef in_order(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n        \n    # 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    in_order(node.left)\n    \n    # 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    print(node.val, end=" ")\n    \n    # 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    in_order(node.right)\n',"Java",'class Node {\n    int val;\n    Node left, right;\n    public Node(int item) { val = item; }\n}\n\nclass TreeTraversal {\n    public void inOrder(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        \n        // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n        inOrder(node.left);\n        \n        // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n        System.out.print(node.val + " ");\n        \n        // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n        inOrder(node.right);\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int val;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n    Node(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}\n};\n\nvoid inOrder(Node* node) {\n    if (node == NULL) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    inOrder(node->left);\n    \n    // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    cout << node->val << " ";\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    inOrder(node->right);\n}\n',"JavaScript","class Node {\n    constructor(val) {\n        this.val = val;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction inOrder(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    inOrder(node.left);\n    \n    // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    console.log(node.val);\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    inOrder(node.right);\n}\n"],a0,a0),p,q,"In-order Traversal is a Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm that processes the tree symmetrically. The golden rule is: Left \u2794 Root \u2794 Right. It plunges down the left side of the tree, processes the node, and then checks the right. This specific order gives it a superpower: when executed on a Binary Search Tree, it retrieves every single piece of data in perfectly sorted, ascending order without needing an expensive sorting algorithm!","tree-in-order-traversal",m,k,A.a(["Assuming it Sorts ANY Tree: In-order traversal only yields sorted data if the underlying tree is a valid Binary Search Tree (BST). Running it on a standard Binary Tree or a Heap just outputs a seemingly random order.","Missing the Base Case: Omitting the 'if (node == null) return;' check will immediately crash your traversal when it hits the bottom of the tree.","Modifying While Traversing: Attempting to insert or delete nodes in the middle of an In-order traversal is extremely dangerous and will severely corrupt the tree structure."],d),a,A.a([A.H(1,"The prefix 'In' means the Root is processed IN the middle. Therefore, the sequence is Left, then Root, then Right.",A.a([g,f,h,e],d),"What is the exact processing sequence for an In-order Traversal?"),A.H(2,"Because a BST stores smaller values to the left and larger to the right, visiting Left \u2794 Root \u2794 Right naturally processes the values from smallest to largest.",A.a(["It safely deletes all nodes from memory.","It creates an exact structural clone of the tree.","It retrieves all values from a Binary Search Tree in sorted (ascending) order.","It evaluates mathematical expressions in Postfix notation."],d),"What is the most famous property of an In-order traversal?"),A.H(2,"A Min-Heap only guarantees that parents are smaller than children. It does not enforce that left children are smaller than right children. Therefore, In-order traversal on a Heap will NOT output sorted data.",A.a(["Yes, Heaps are a type of sorted tree.","Yes, In-order traversal sorts all binary trees.","No, Heaps only guarantee the root is the minimum, not a strict left-right ordering.","No, Heaps require Pre-order traversal to be sorted."],d),"If you perform an In-order traversal on a Min-Heap, will the output be sorted?"),A.H(1,"By simply swapping the recursive calls (visiting the Right side first, then the Root, then the Left side), you process the largest values first, printing the tree in reverse sorted order!",A.a(["You can't. You must store the result in an array and reverse it.","Change the order to Right Subtree \u2794 Root \u2794 Left Subtree.","Use Post-order traversal instead.","Use Level-order traversal instead."],d),"How would you modify an In-order traversal to print a Binary Search Tree in DESCENDING (largest to smallest) order?")],a1),"assets/mds/dsa/trees/in_order.md",B.n5)
