q=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef post_order(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n        \n    # 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    post_order(node.left)\n    \n    # 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    post_order(node.right)\n    \n    # 3. Visit Root LAST\n    print(node.val, end=" ")\n',"Java",'class Node {\n    int val;\n    Node left, right;\n    public Node(int item) { val = item; }\n}\n\nclass TreeTraversal {\n    public void postOrder(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        \n        // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n        postOrder(node.left);\n        \n        // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n        postOrder(node.right);\n        \n        // 3. Visit Root LAST\n        System.out.print(node.val + " ");\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int val;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n    Node(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}\n};\n\nvoid postOrder(Node* node) {\n    if (node == NULL) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    postOrder(node->left);\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    postOrder(node->right);\n    \n    // 3. Visit Root LAST\n    cout << node->val << " ";\n}\n',"JavaScript","class Node {\n    constructor(val) {\n        this.val = val;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction postOrder(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    postOrder(node.left);\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    postOrder(node.right);\n    \n    // 3. Visit Root LAST\n    console.log(node.val);\n}\n"],a0,a0),o,p,"Post-order Traversal is the ultimate 'Bottom-Up' Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm. The golden rule is: Left \u2794 Right \u2794 Root. It plunges all the way down to the deepest leaves, processes them, and then works its way backward up to the Root. This makes it the undisputed champion algorithm for safely deleting a tree from memory, as you can never delete a parent until you have successfully deleted all of its children.","tree-post-order-traversal",m,j,A.a(["Iterative Implementation Complexity: Writing Post-order iteratively (without recursion) is notoriously difficult compared to Pre-order and In-order, often requiring two Stacks or complex pointer tracking.","Misunderstanding the Root's Role: Thinking 'processing last' means the algorithm doesn't 'see' the root until the end. The algorithm physically passes through the root to get to the children, but delays *executing its logic* until the way back up.","Accidental Un-linking: Deleting a node's children but forgetting to set the parent's `left` or `right` pointers to `null`, causing the parent to point to garbage memory."],d),q,A.a([A.H(3,"The prefix 'Post' means 'After'. Therefore, the Root is processed AFTER traversing both its Left and Right children.",A.a([g,f,e,h],d),"What is the exact processing sequence for a Post-order Traversal?"),A.H(1,"If you delete a parent first, you lose the pointers to its children forever, causing a memory leak. Post-order guarantees children are deleted before their parent.",A.a(["Cloning a tree structure exactly.","Deleting an entire tree from memory in languages like C++.","Printing the values of a BST in ascending order.","Finding the shortest path between a node and the root."],d),"Which real-world scenario is Post-order traversal strictly required for?"),A.H(2,"Post-order outputs Reverse Polish Notation (Postfix). Compilers love Postfix because it completely eliminates the need for parentheses when evaluating equations.",A.a(["Infix Notation (A + B * C)","Prefix Notation (* + A B C)","Postfix Notation (A B + C *)","Binary Notation"],d),"When you apply Post-order traversal to a Math Expression Tree (like `(A+B)*C`), what is the resulting output format?"),A.H(3,"Because the algorithm dictates Left \u2794 Right \u2794 Root at every level, the main Root of the entire tree will wait for every single other node to be processed before it finally processes itself.",A.a(["The leftmost leaf node.","The rightmost leaf node.","The deepest node in the tree.","The main Root of the entire tree."],d),"In a Post-order traversal of a tree, which node is ALWAYS processed absolutely last?")],a1),"assets/mds/dsa/trees/post_order.md",B.n5)
