g=A.bI(A.a1(["Python","class GraphMatrix:\n    def __init__(self, num_vertices):\n        self.V = num_vertices\n        # Initialize a 2D array of 0s\n        self.matrix = [[0] * self.V for _ in range(self.V)]\n\n    def add_edge(self, v1, v2):\n        # 1 means an edge exists. \n        # (Change to a weight value for Weighted Graphs)\n        self.matrix[v1][v2] = 1\n        self.matrix[v2][v1] = 1  # For Undirected Graph\n\n    def print_matrix(self):\n        for row in self.matrix:\n            print(row)\n","Java","class GraphMatrix {\n    private int[][] matrix;\n    private int V;\n\n    public GraphMatrix(int numVertices) {\n        V = numVertices;\n        matrix = new int[V][V]; // Defaults to 0s in Java\n    }\n\n    public void addEdge(int v1, int v2) {\n        matrix[v1][v2] = 1;\n        matrix[v2][v1] = 1; // For Undirected Graph\n    }\n\n    public boolean hasEdge(int v1, int v2) {\n        return matrix[v1][v2] == 1;\n    }\n}\n","C++","#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass GraphMatrix {\nprivate:\n    vector<vector<int>> matrix;\n    int V;\n\npublic:\n    GraphMatrix(int numVertices) {\n        V = numVertices;\n        // Initialize V x V matrix with 0s\n        matrix.assign(V, vector<int>(V, 0));\n    }\n\n    void addEdge(int v1, int v2) {\n        matrix[v1][v2] = 1;\n        matrix[v2][v1] = 1; // For Undirected Graph\n    }\n};\n","JavaScript","class GraphMatrix {\n    constructor(numVertices) {\n        this.V = numVertices;\n        // Create 2D array filled with 0s\n        this.matrix = Array(this.V).fill().map(() => Array(this.V).fill(0));\n    }\n\n    addEdge(v1, v2) {\n        this.matrix[v1][v2] = 1;\n        this.matrix[v2][v1] = 1; // For Undirected Graph\n    }\n\n    hasEdge(v1, v2) {\n        return this.matrix[v1][v2] === 1;\n    }\n}\n"],e,e),q,f,"An Adjacency Matrix is the simplest way to represent a Graph: it's just a 2D Array (a grid). The rows represent the starting nodes, the columns represent the ending nodes, and the cells hold a '1' if an edge connects them, or a '0' if they are disconnected. While it uses a massive amount of memory (O(V\xb2)), it provides unmatched speed (O(1)) for checking if a specific connection exists. It is the absolute weapon of choice for Dense Graphs.","graph-adjacency-matrix",m,"Graph Adjacency Matrix",A.a(["Memory Limit Exceeded (MLE): The #1 reason beginners fail Graph questions on LeetCode. If a problem states V <= 10^5, an Adjacency Matrix will crash your program. You MUST use an Adjacency List.","0-Indexed vs 1-Indexed Nodes: Most programming arrays start at 0, but graph problems often label nodes from 1 to N. Forgetting to subtract 1 (e.g., matrix[v1 - 1][v2 - 1]) causes Out-of-Bounds errors.","Directed Graph Mistakes: For a Directed Graph (one-way street from A to B), you only set matrix[A][B] = 1. Setting matrix[B][A] = 1 by habit will ruin the graph's logic."],i),g,A.a([A.H(2,"An adjacency matrix requires a 2D array of size V by V. Therefore, the memory grows quadratically relative to the number of vertices, resulting in O(V\xb2) space.",A.a(["O(V)","O(V + E)","O(V\xb2)","O(E\xb2)"],i),"What is the Space Complexity of an Adjacency Matrix representing a graph with V vertices?"),A.H(0,"It is an instant O(1) operation! Because it's a standard 2D array, you can directly access the memory address by looking up matrix[A][B] without looping.",A.a(["O(1)","O(log V)","O(V)","O(E)"],i),"How fast is checking if an edge exists between Node A and Node B using an Adjacency Matrix?"),A.H(2,"In an undirected graph, if A connects to B, B connects to A. This means matrix[A][B] will equal matrix[B][A], making the matrix symmetrical across its diagonal.",A.a(["All values will be 1.","The main diagonal (top-left to bottom-right) will be entirely 1s.","It will be perfectly symmetrical across the main diagonal.","The sum of all rows will equal 0."],i),"If you are representing an UNDIRECTED graph, what visual property will the Adjacency Matrix always have?"),A.H(2,"For a sparse graph of 100,000 nodes, you would allocate 10 Billion cells of memory (likely crashing the program) just to store 5,000 actual connections. An Adjacency List is far better here.",A.a(["When the graph is extremely dense (almost all nodes are connected).","When you need to frequently check if a specific edge exists.","When you have 100,000 nodes but only 5,000 edges (Sparse Graph).","When edge weights need to be updated frequently."],i),"In which scenario is it a TERRIBLE idea to use an Adjacency Matrix?")],d),"assets/mds/dsa/graphs/adjacency_matrix.md",B.Jl)
