f=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'class WeightedDirectedGraph:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Adjacency List storing tuples: (Neighbor, Weight)\n        self.adj = {}\n\n    def add_edge(self, u, v, weight):\n        if u not in self.adj: self.adj[u] = []\n        if v not in self.adj: self.adj[v] = []\n        \n        # Directed: u -> v. Weighted: weight\n        self.adj[u].append((v, weight))\n        \n        # If there is a self-loop: u == v\n        if u == v:\n            print(f"Self-loop added at {u} with cost {weight}")\n',"Java","import java.util.*;\n\nclass Edge {\n    int target;\n    int weight;\n    public Edge(int target, int weight) {\n        this.target = target;\n        this.weight = weight;\n    }\n}\n\nclass WeightedDirectedGraph {\n    Map<Integer, List<Edge>> adj = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public void addEdge(int u, int v, int weight) {\n        adj.putIfAbsent(u, new ArrayList<>());\n        adj.putIfAbsent(v, new ArrayList<>());\n        \n        // Directed & Weighted Edge\n        adj.get(u).add(new Edge(v, weight));\n    }\n}\n","C++","#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass WeightedDirectedGraph {\npublic:\n    // unordered_map where key is node, value is vector of pairs (Neighbor, Weight)\n    unordered_map<int, vector<pair<int, int>>> adj;\n\n    void addEdge(int u, int v, int weight) {\n        // Directed: u -> v only\n        adj[u].push_back({v, weight});\n    }\n};\n","JavaScript","class WeightedDirectedGraph {\n    constructor() {\n        // Map to store node -> [{node: neighbor, weight: cost}]\n        this.adj = new Map();\n    }\n\n    addEdge(u, v, weight) {\n        if (!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u, []);\n        if (!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v, []);\n        \n        this.adj.get(u).push({ node: v, weight: weight });\n    }\n}\n"],e,e),p,q,"To use Graphs in the real world, you need to understand their properties. **Weighted** graphs assign a cost to edges (like toll roads or flight distances). **Directed** graphs restrict movement to one-way arrows (like Twitter follows). **Connected** graphs ensure no nodes are isolated on an island. A **Cycle** occurs when you can loop back to your starting point, and a **Self-Loop** is a node that directly points to itself. Mastering these properties unlocks the ability to use advanced AI algorithms like Dijkstra's Shortest Path and Topological Sorting.","graph-properties",m,"Graph Properties (Weighted & Directed)",A.a(["Confusing BFS with Dijkstra: Using standard BFS to find the shortest path on a Weighted Graph. BFS only finds the shortest path by *number of edges*, not by total weight. You MUST use Dijkstra's.","Undirected Cycle Detection Flaw: In an Undirected graph, simply visiting an already-visited node doesn't mean there's a cycle\u2014it might just be the node you just came from! You must keep track of the 'parent' node to verify true cycles.","Strong vs. Weak Connectivity: In Directed Graphs, being 'Weakly Connected' means the graph would be connected if you ignored the arrows. 'Strongly Connected' means you can reach EVERY node from EVERY OTHER node following the arrows."],i),f,A.a([A.H(2,"Dijkstra's Algorithm uses a Priority Queue (Min-Heap) to constantly explore the cheapest available path, making it perfect for weighted graphs.",A.a([l,k,j,"Kruskal's Algorithm"],i),"Which algorithm is the industry standard for finding the shortest path in a Weighted Graph (assuming there are no negative weights)?"),A.H(1,"If a cycle has a negative total weight, every time the algorithm loops through it, the total 'cost' goes down. The algorithm will loop infinitely trying to reach negative infinity. You must use Bellman-Ford to detect this.",A.a(["It finds the shortest path instantly.","It will get stuck in an infinite loop reducing the cost forever.","It throws a Null Pointer Exception.","It ignores the negative weight and treats it as a 0."],i),"What happens if you run Dijkstra's Algorithm on a graph that contains a Negative Weight Cycle?"),A.H(0,"Directed (one-way arrows) + Acyclic (no loops). DAGs are incredibly important in computer science because they are the only graphs that can be 'Topologically Sorted' (used for scheduling tasks with prerequisites).",A.a(["A graph with one-way edges and absolutely no loops/cycles.","A graph where all edges have a weight of 0.","A graph where every node connects to every other node.","An undirected graph with cycles."],i),"What is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)?"),A.H(2,"Strong connectivity means you can pick any node A and any node B, and you are guaranteed to find a path from A to B, AND a path from B to A following the arrows.",A.a(["The graph has a very high number of edges.","Every node is connected to a central 'Root' node.","There is a valid path from EVERY node to EVERY OTHER node.","The graph would be connected if you removed all the directional arrows."],i),"What defines a 'Strongly Connected' Directed Graph?")],d),"assets/mds/dsa/graphs/properties.md",B.Ut)
q=A.a(["Breadth-First Search (BFS): Guaranteed to find the absolute shortest path (fewest number of edges) between a starting node and a target node in an unweighted graph.","Depth-First Search (DFS): Extremely memory efficient for exploring deep, narrow graphs. It is the core algorithm used for Topological Sorting and solving mazes.","Universal Applicability: Every advanced graph algorithm (like Dijkstra's or Kosaraju's) is essentially just a modified version of BFS or DFS."],i)
p=A.a(["BFS Memory Hog: To search level-by-level, BFS must store every node on the current level in its Queue. For very 'wide' graphs, this consumes massive amounts of memory.","DFS Shortest Path Failure: DFS blindly plunges down a single path until it hits a dead end. It will find *a* path to the target, but it rarely finds the *shortest* path.","Stack Overflow (DFS): Because DFS heavily uses system recursion, a graph with an incredibly long, winding path (like 100,000 nodes deep) will crash the program."],i)
o=A.a(["Time Complexity: O(V + E) for both algorithms \u2014 You must visit every Vertex (V) and traverse every Edge (E) to explore the entire graph.","Space Complexity (BFS): O(W) \u2014 Where W is the maximum width of the graph (nodes in the Queue). Worst case O(V).","Space Complexity (DFS): O(H) \u2014 Where H is the maximum depth of the graph (frames on the Call Stack). Worst case O(V)."],i)
q=A.bI(A.a1(["Python",'# Assuming \'graph\' is an Adjacency List dictionary\n\ndef bfs(graph, start):\n    visited = set([start])\n    queue = [start]\n    \n    while queue:\n        curr = queue.pop(0) # FIFO\n        print(curr, end=" ")\n        \n        for neighbor in graph[curr]:\n            if neighbor not in visited:\n                visited.add(neighbor)\n                queue.append(neighbor)\n\ndef dfs(graph, curr, visited=None):\n    if visited is None: visited = set()\n    \n    visited.add(curr)\n    print(curr, end=" ")\n    \n    for neighbor in graph[curr]:\n        if neighbor not in visited:\n            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited)\n',"Java",'import java.util.*;\n\nclass GraphTraversal {\n    public void bfs(Map<String, List<String>> graph, String start) {\n        Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>();\n        Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        \n        visited.add(start);\n        queue.add(start);\n        \n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            String curr = queue.poll();\n            System.out.print(curr + " ");\n            \n            for (String neighbor : graph.getOrDefault(curr, new ArrayList<>())) {\n                if (!visited.contains(neighbor)) {\n                    visited.add(neighbor);\n                    queue.add(neighbor);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void dfs(Map<String, List<String>> graph, String curr, Set<String> visited) {\n        visited.add(curr);\n        System.out.print(curr + " ");\n        \n        for (String neighbor : graph.getOrDefault(curr, new ArrayList<>())) {\n            if (!visited.contains(neighbor)) {\n                dfs(graph, neighbor, visited);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <unordered_set>\n#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid bfs(unordered_map<string, vector<string>>& graph, string start) {\n    unordered_set<string> visited;\n    queue<string> q;\n    \n    visited.insert(start);\n    q.push(start);\n    \n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        string curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        cout << curr << " ";\n        \n        for (string neighbor : graph[curr]) {\n            if (visited.find(neighbor) == visited.end()) {\n                visited.insert(neighbor);\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvoid dfs(unordered_map<string, vector<string>>& graph, string curr, unordered_set<string>& visited) {\n    visited.insert(curr);\n    cout << curr << " ";\n    \n    for (string neighbor : graph[curr]) {\n        if (visited.find(neighbor) == visited.end()) {\n            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited);\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"JavaScript","function bfs(graph, start) {\n    let visited = new Set([start]);\n    let queue = [start];\n    \n    while (queue.length > 0) {\n        let curr = queue.shift(); // Remove from front\n        console.log(curr);\n        \n        for (let neighbor of graph[curr]) {\n            if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {\n                visited.add(neighbor);\n                queue.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfunction dfs(graph, curr, visited = new Set()) {\n    visited.add(curr);\n    console.log(curr);\n    \n    for (let neighbor of graph[curr]) {\n        if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {\n            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited);\n        }\n    }\n}\n"],e,e),o,p,"To extract data from a Graph, you must traverse it. Breadth-First Search (BFS) acts like a shockwave, exploring all immediate neighbors first using a Queue. It is the absolute best way to find the shortest unweighted path (e.g., 'Degrees of separation' on LinkedIn). Depth-First Search (DFS) acts like a maze runner, plunging down a single path as deeply as possible before backtracking. By mastering these two traversals, you unlock the ability to solve almost every graph problem in existence.","graph-traversal-bfs-dfs",m,"Graph Traversals: BFS & DFS",A.a(["The Infinite Cycle: Unlike trees, graphs have loops! If you forget to add a node to your 'Visited' set immediately upon discovering it, your algorithm will bounce between two connected nodes infinitely.","Using the Wrong Strategy: Trying to use DFS to find the shortest path in a maze. DFS might find a path that is 500 steps long before it finds the 10-step path. Always use BFS for shortest-path unweighted problems.","Mixing up Stack and Queue: BFS strictly requires a Queue (First-In, First-Out). DFS requires a Stack (Last-In, First-Out, usually handled automatically by recursion). Using the wrong one completely breaks the traversal logic."],i),q,A.a([A.H(1,"BFS relies on a Queue (First-In, First-Out) to ensure that nodes discovered first (closer to the start) are explored before nodes discovered later.",A.a(["Stack (LIFO)","Queue (FIFO)","Hash Map","Min-Heap"],i),"Which data structure is the backbone of a Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm?"),A.H(1,"BFS explores level-by-level outward from the starting point. The very first time it touches the exit, you are mathematically guaranteed it took the shortest possible route.",A.a([k,l,"In-order Traversal","Pre-order Traversal"],i),"If you need to find the absolute shortest path out of a maze (unweighted graph), which traversal MUST you use?"),A.H(2,"Trees flow downward without looping. Graphs often contain Cycles (A connects to B, B connects to A). Without a 'Visited' set, algorithms will bounce between cycles infinitely.",A.a(["A Root Node tracker.","A While Loop.","A 'Visited' Set.","An Adjacency Matrix."],i),"What critical component is required for Graph Traversal that isn't required for Tree Traversal?"),A.H(2,"DFS relies on Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) logic. While you can write it iteratively using an explicit Stack, it is almost always implemented elegantly using standard Recursion, which utilizes the system's underlying Call Stack.",A.a(["Using a standard array.","By pushing nodes to a Queue.","By utilizing the System Call Stack via Recursion.","By creating copies of the graph in memory."],i),"How does Depth-First Search (DFS) typically manage its memory?")],d),"assets/mds/dsa/graphs/traversals.md",B.wk)
